+## Verdict
+
+**CONDITIONAL SUPPORT — I would vote YES if fix conditions F1–F7 below are incorporated into the bill text before ballot solicitation.** I decline if any pre-launch efficacy, calibration, attestation or "PROCEED" gate is reintroduced, if the parallel four-office authorization is removed without a later exact-author Founder instruction, if the cumulative 45-day `UPPER_BOUND` is made resettable, or if the Act 16 §2 assumptions schedule remains unfiled.
+
+**Why support rather than decline.** The final bill is a genuine architectural improvement on both earlier drafts. It removes the circular pre-launch gates (`§2(1)`, `§2(3)`), authorizes all four offices in parallel (`§2(1)`), makes the adaptive mandates real and non-canned (`§2(2)`), explicitly says fixed telemetry is not an institution or an outcome (`§1`, `§3.4`), preserves candour that the 20-second OpenCode limiter is not a strict per-request cap (`§3.2`), keeps cumulative liability non-resettable (`§3.3`), puts the Judiciary review 48 h **after** start with fail-closed suspension (`§4.1`), protects Post/Spying/audit separation (`§3.5`), and forbids enact-by-operator and model pinning (`§6`). On the central Founder correction — "don't do small" — the bill is on the right side. The objections below are about whether these promises are **enforceable** and **filed**, not about reversing the design.
+
+**The strongest single case against it:** as drafted, the bill can be complied with while producing four fixed-report "institutions", unbounded model billing under a candidly-admitted non-cap, and no Act 16 §2 assumptions schedule at all — and §5's falsifiers would not fire until after a review window. Each of these is fixable with short clauses that do not restore any prelaunch gate.
+
+---
+
+## O1 — Adaptive institution vs. fixed telemetry can be declared compliant without ever exercising adaptive judgment
+
+**Sources.** `sha256:rzlgEqJQ…` (07:21Z): "statistics bureau must not be a set of fixed metrics… The fixed `statistics-report.py` outputs are auxiliary telemetry only; they NEVER count as an operational Statistics Bureau or demonstrate its value." `sha256:onFmcp0bRY…`: "Do NOT equate operational Bureau with fixed canned metrics." `sha256:BSTZyiNhj…`: "no single permit may rely on a model-free canned report as an operating Bureau. Fixed telemetry is auxiliary only."
+
+**What the bill gets right.** `§1`, `§2(2)` and `§3.4` already say the right things: `auxiliary_telemetry: true`, "never an operating institution, an outcome, or evidence of marginal value", "A fixed report never satisfies a Founder design falsifier."
+
+**Defect.** These are declarations, not conditions on dispatch. `§2(1)(c)` requires only an "operating-budget permit (§3)" — it never requires the permit to describe *how* each office will exercise adaptive selection. `§4.1` asks the Judiciary to review "real output, safety and faults" but not to determine whether an office actually selected, revised, retired or invented a question/forecast/decision/source in the window. `§5`'s falsifier ("no independently checked outcome with a resource denominator") is a *post-window* test. The predictable failure is four "activated" offices publishing `statistics-report.py` output and `post.py` receipts, a "success" narrative built from publication counts, and no adaptive behaviour — exactly the artefact the Founder called a FAILURE. The bill's own `§4(3)` ("publication counts, agent exits, merges and clean reports are never outcomes") has no enforcement hook.
+
+**Fix F1 (before ballot).** Add to `§2(1)(c)`:
+> Each permit MUST record a per-office **adaptive-mechanism declaration**: for Statistics, an inquiry ledger recording question, `why_now`, `decision_use`, source validation and at least one revised or retired question; for Foresight, competing conditional trajectories with every scoreable binary forecast hash-bound to a committed Act 22 registration; for Strategic Development, a living decision portfolio with status-quo comparator, alternatives, cost, reversibility and intervention/outcome separation; for Spying, an allowlisted source log with competing explanations and preserved inference errors. A deterministic/no-provider path is recorded `auxiliary_telemetry: true` and satisfies none of these. Section 4(1)'s review MUST include a source-linked finding on whether the office actually exercised adaptive selection; a fixed report, a process count or a clean readiness report is non-compliance, not evidence.
+
+---
+
+## O2 — Candour on the non-hard-cap is preserved in text but not made a condition of any permit
+
+**Sources.** `sha256:bYOBx9IQ…` (2026-11-22T09:40Z): a 400000-token reservation "is not itself proof"; give the negative test and maximum possible overrun, or mark stop-on-detection and hold. `sha256:fKiRF4a…` (07:17Z): "Prior 400k in-flight OpenCode spending remains strictly unbounded; NO model-driven office may rely on that nominal cap." `sha256:rzlgEqJQ…`: "The current unsafe OpenCode per-request token cap STILL blocks authorized model-using office activation." `sha256:BSTZyiNhj…` (07:46Z): "no claim that retrospective OpenCode polling is a strict per-request billing cap. Economy and Judiciary still prove any model-using cost bound."
+
+**What the bill gets right.** `§3.2` is admirably candid: the limiter "is **not** a strict per-request hard cap"; continuations, retries and counter lag can exceed a reservation; "Do not claim a mathematical hard cap on one request." I credit this explicitly — it is the opposite of a token compliance finding.
+
+**Defect.** `§2(3)` says "Adaptive **model usage is permitted** subject to §3 budgets. No requirement to prove … a mathematically strict per-request cap before operating." Read with `§3.2`'s "Where a provider or gateway enforces a verifiable per-request limit, use it", a permit can assert a model-using office, rely entirely on retrospective stop-on-detection ceilings, and never state whether an enforceable bound exists. The Founder's 07:46Z condition — Economy **and** Judiciary still prove any model-using cost bound — is not in the bill. "The Executive sets … budgets" (`§3.1`) is a self-authorization, not independent proof on the exact bytes to be deployed. Nothing mechanically prevents a later report or ballot material from claiming a "strict cap" that `§3.2` disclaims. The honest reconciliation of the two Founder instructions is: **parallel authorization need not wait, but model dispatch must wait for an independently proven enforceable cost bound**; until then the office may run zero-model and its output is auxiliary telemetry.
+
+**Fix F2 (before ballot for the text; before any model-using dispatch for the proof).** Add to `§3.2`:
+> Every permit MUST declare `model_mode: none | bounded`. `none` issues no Antonina/OpenCode/provider request and its output is auxiliary telemetry only. A `bounded` permit MUST NOT issue any model dispatch until Economy and the independent Judiciary have signed, against the exact deployed source bytes, verification of an enforceable operating-budget ceiling covering provider-enforced per-request limits where available, no continuation after kill/cancel, retries, counter lag/reset/missing-counter handling, bounded concurrency, and the aggregate 45-day `UPPER_BOUND` ledger. No permit, report, ballot material or board claim may assert a strict per-request billing cap unless that proof exists; the known 20-second retrospective limiter is stop-on-detection only. If no bound is proven, the office proceeds in `none` mode without delaying the parallel authorization of the other offices.
+
