+
+## O3 — "Bounded risk" lacks numeric ceilings, independent verification, and working STOP mechanics
+
+**Sources.** `sha256:onFmcp0bRY…`: "choose enforceable operating-budget ceilings and bounded concurrency, STOP/REASSESS if impossible." `sha256:gz5uKJVkk7qAL…` (09:14Z): two separate ledgers, safety `UPPER_BOUND` charges accumulate and "must never be bypassed or reset by rebranding a new trial"; value accounting keeps per-office CPU `UNAVAILABLE`. Act 33 §3 (LAWS.md): unknown meter state blocks dispatch; confirmed overrun requires fail-closed stop/wind-down.
+
+**What the bill gets right.** `§3.1` names token/wall/CPU ceilings, bounded concurrency, cancellation and detect-and-kill, and STOP/REASSESS. `§3.3` is excellent: `UPPER_BOUND` "accumulate[s] on the unchanged 45-day Economy ledger and are never reset by restarting, renaming or expanding a pilot", with actual per-office CPU `UNAVAILABLE` and fail-closed wind-down. I would vote against any amendment weakening `§3.3`.
+
+**Defect.** No numeric ceiling is stated or required in the permit; "per office and in aggregate" is not defined for four simultaneous offices, so four individually compliant offices can collectively exhaust the host. No requirement that ceilings be independently verified on exact bytes, that unknown/missing/stale meter state blocks dispatch, that kill actually prevents continuation and billing, that STOP/REASSESS mechanically suspends rather than being a journal entry, or that reboot, expiry, deadline and overrun negative tests are passed. Economy's 2026-11-22 finding (40-token limit continued to ~252 tokens; `admit(399000)=true`) is precisely the failure mode this section must close.
+
+**Fix F3 (before ballot for the text; before dispatch for verification).** Add to `§3.1`:
+> Each permit MUST state numeric token, wall-time, CPU and concurrency ceilings for its office **and** a single aggregate ceiling across all concurrently authorized offices with explicit headroom. Dispatch is blocked when any meter is unknown, missing or stale. Cancellation and detect-and-kill MUST be verified on the exact deployed bytes to prevent continuation or further billing after the kill signal; a kill that is merely advisory is not enforcement. STOP/REASSESS MUST mechanically suspend dispatch until a new permit and preserve evidence. Reboot, expiry, deadline, counter-reset and confirmed-overrun negative tests MUST pass before a ceiling is relied on. Section 3(3)'s `UPPER_BOUND` ledger is unchanged: exceeding the aggregate ceiling by any office or combination of offices stops all four, and no restart, rename, rebranding or expansion resets the accumulated liability.
+
